Change (non-test lines only; no test is added or changed). Every hunk replaces code that the
translation cannot handle by calls to the new module `prim` or by an equivalent expression, and
keeps the computed values:
 prim  New private module `prim` at the end of mcp_headers.rs (before `mod tests`), opaque in
       the translation and modelled in Lean, each the original expression with the same
       arguments: has_control_or_non_ascii (`value.chars().any(|c| (c as u32) < 0x20 ||
       (c as u32) > 0x7E)`), starts_with (`s.starts_with(prefix)`), ends_with
       (`s.ends_with(suffix)`), strip_prefix (`s.strip_prefix(prefix)`), strip_suffix
       (`s.strip_suffix(suffix)`), all with &str patterns; wrap_sentinel
       (`format!("{BASE64_HEADER_PREFIX}{encoded}{BASE64_HEADER_SUFFIX}")`), base64_encode
       (`BASE64_STANDARD.encode(value)`) and base64_decode (`BASE64_STANDARD.decode(value).ok()`).
       The `use base64::{Engine, prelude::BASE64_STANDARD}` of G03 and G04 moves into prim. Used
       by M1, M3 and M4.
 M1  G01 requires_base64: `bytes.first().copied()` and `bytes.last().copied()` in the two
     `matches!` (instead of matching `Some(b' ' | b'\t')` on the `Option<&u8>`), the
     `chars().any(..)` test becomes `prim::has_control_or_non_ascii(value)`, and
     starts_with/ends_with become `prim::starts_with`/`prim::ends_with`. rustfmt splits the
     `if` condition over three lines.
 M2  G02 is_tchar: the `matches!(c, '!' | '#' | ... | '~')` over the fifteen token
     punctuation chars becomes a `||` chain of `c == '!'`, `c == '#'`, ... in the same order;
     the quote is compared as `c == char::from(b'\'')`, with a comment saying why (GD1).
 M3  G03 encode_header_value: `prim::wrap_sentinel(&prim::base64_encode(value))` for the
     `format!` of the sentinel around `BASE64_STANDARD.encode(value)`.
 M4  G04 decode_header_value: `value.strip_prefix(..).and_then(|inner|
     inner.strip_suffix(..))` becomes two nested matches on `prim::strip_prefix` and
     `prim::strip_suffix`, with the `None` arm `Some(value.to_owned())` written for each of the
     two; `prim::base64_decode(inner)?` for `BASE64_STANDARD.decode(inner).ok()?`.

Why each hunk is needed (measured 2026-11-27 in a scratch copy with the Charon settings and
scripts above; each error is what Charon, Aeneas or the Lean build reports without the hunk):
 prim, M1, M4  "[Error] Unimplemented" on the signatures of str::starts_with, ends_with,
     strip_prefix and strip_suffix (the core::str::pattern::Pattern trait has a generic
     associated type, as patches 04-06); `str::chars().any` with a closure and the `c as u32`
     casts (see M2); the format! and the base64 Engine would need models of alloc::fmt::format,
     core::fmt::Arguments and the Engine trait (the first run, with none of the hunks, also stopped
     with "Fatal error: exception Aeneas.Errors.CFailure").
 M1  "[Error] Internal error, please file an issue" on the `matches!` of `bytes.first()` and
     `bytes.last()`: the pattern `Some(b' ' | b'\t')` is a switch on the byte behind the
     reference in the `Some`.
 M2  "[Error] Unsupported switch scrutinee" on the `matches!` over a `char`. The quote needs
     the workaround because of two Aeneas defects (reproducers kept outside the repository,
     2026-11-27, Aeneas nightly-2026.11.24-fb44cb4 and Charon 0.1.324): (1) a one-function crate
     `fn is_quote(c: char) -> bool { c == '\'' }` translates to `ok (c = ''')`, and
     `c == '\\'` to `ok (c = '\')`: Aeneas prints the char literal without escaping it, which
     Lean rejects (in the rmcp translation: "unexpected token '''; expected term" at the
     comparison in is_tchar); (2) the obvious alternative `c as u32 == 0x27` makes Aeneas fail
     with "[Error] Unreachable" on the cast. `char::from(b'\'')` translates (to
     `Char.Insts.CoreConvertFromU8.from 39#u8`, an existing exact model; `u32::from(c) == 0x27`
     also translates but would need a new model). Remove the workaround when Aeneas escapes char literals.
 M4  "[Error] Can't end abstraction ... as it is set as non-endable" on the closure passed to
     and_then; a join `let inner = match .. { .. }` instead of the duplicated `None` arm gives
     "[Error] Nested borrows are not supported yet".
No upstream Charon or Aeneas issue has been filed (as of 2026-11-27).

Why the behaviour is unchanged: every value computed is the same and in the same order. The prim
functions are the original expressions with the same arguments (the same &str patterns, the same
format string with the encoded value as an inline argument instead of a positional one, the same
BASE64_STANDARD engine); `bytes.first().copied()` is `Some(b)` exactly when `bytes.first()` is
`Some(&b)`, so each `matches!` holds for the same values; `c == x1 || .. || c == x15` is the
`matches!` on the same fifteen chars, and `char::from(b'\'')` is `'\''` (U+0027, `u8 as char`);
`a.and_then(f)` is `match a { Some(x) => f(x), None => None }`, and both `None` cases of the
nested match return `Some(value.to_owned())` as the single `None` arm did; `?` on the Option
returned by prim::base64_decode is the `?` on `BASE64_STANDARD.decode(inner).ok()`.
Warnings: patch 07 adds no warning: cargo clippy -p rmcp --lib --no-default-features --features
request-state,server,client,client-side-sse,transport-streamable-http-server,
transport-streamable-http-client,auth (the translated feature configuration), with and without
the feature macros, reports the same set of warnings (same lint, file and message) on the
patch-01..06 copy and on the patch-01..07 copy (counts in formal/trusted/evidence/README.md). The
crate does not deny warnings.
Tests: scripts/apply-patches.sh deletes Cargo.lock in the copy, so the workspace Cargo.lock is
copied into it (or cargo generate-lockfile is run there) before cargo test --locked -p rmcp --lib
--no-default-features --features request-state,server,client,client-side-sse,
